In article <33df9c1e.0@amhnt2.amherst.edu>, Paul D. Chapin <pdchapin@unix.amherst.edu> wrote:
> Paul D. Chapin (pdchapin@unix.amherst.edu) wrote:
> : Does anybody know if DEC is even trying to get their enhanced
> : security option to work with a large user base?

> To be more specific, the problem when using it with NIS where there is
> a bottleneck with the protected password database.  What's really
> annoying is that they've solved the basic problem since enhanced
> security now uses a single data base.

Having been down this ugly road myself, I offer my thoughts.

The latest Digital Unix SPD still says:

"Network Information Service (NIS) Compatibility 

   Support is provided for accessing NIS distributed databases while run ning 
Enhanced Security. NIS can also be used to distribute the Enhanced Security
protected password database. When using NIS, no more than 4000 user accounts 
are supported by the Enhanced Security subset. The num ber of simultaneous
logins allowed is dependent on the configuration."

That statement appeared in the SPD around the time that Digital was
deeply emeshed in trying to fix NIS/C2 problems here, and essentially
closed the book on them.

This is not a Release Notes implementation restriction. This is a formal
statement of product features.

NIS/C2 is just not supported for large sites. We consider making technical
plans that includes these feature to be irresponsible, and simply
assume that they will never be there. The only thing that would change
my mind about that would be to see that statement dropped from the
SPD, a matching statement in the Release Notes stating exactly what is
supported, _and_ a shipping software version that actually works.

	- Saul, who was very badly burned by these and related problems

