[ Mitzi Adams ]

>>  Several weeks back, I posted a question about whether it was necessary
>>  to by additional hard drives (of 9 Gig size) from Dec or whether, for
>>  instance, a Seagate would work. At that time, I was not aware that our
>>  system was purchased with an Adaptec SCSI controller card for an EISA
>>  bus.  We purchased a Seagate 9G drive, have tried to install it, and
>>  have had nothing but trouble.  We not only had difficulties in mounting
>>  and partitioning the drive, but it doesn't seem to want to run properly,
>>  i.e., it will hang the system.  Has anyone had similar problems?  Could
>>  the problem be with the EISA bus and/or controller card?  

(I've added comp.periphs.scsi and comp.sys.dec to the newsgroups, in hopes
that it might reach a broader set of knowledgeable people.)

We've had problems too numerous to mention with the Adaptec SCSI controllers
that live in the EISA bus on our AlphaServer 2100 systems.  When we purchased
these systems (approximately three years ago), we knew that we'd need to hook
up a lot of disks, so we specified that the systems should include a second
SCSI controller.  The EISA-bus Adaptec is what Digital supplied.

I won't bore you with all the details, but the executive summary is that many
disks tend to hang the bus:  Some disks are totally unusable from the moment
they're installed, whereas others will work OK for a few days, or even weeks
or months, and then will hang the bus.

It's not a case of a too-long SCSI cable, or bad termination, or anything of
the sort.  For example, we have a couple of 9 GByte drives which don't work
even if they're the only device on the bus, with a short SCSI cable (and we
tried both passive terminators and active terminators).

These same drives work just fine on

     all non-DEC systems we've tried (e.g. Sun, SGI);
and
     all of our older DEC Alphas (e.g. DEC 3000 model 500);
and
     these same AlphaServer machines, if plugged into the
     first (i.e. non-Adaptec) SCSI bus!

We've seen these problems with 9 GByte drives from both Seagate and Fujitsu,
and also with some 4 GByte Seagate drives.  We have no easy way to determine
the culprit:  Is it a problem with the disks?  (Seems unlikely, as they work
flawlessly on so many other machines.)  Some problem with the Adaptec card?
(But aren't these same cards used on zillions of PCs?)  Something wrong with
whatever DEC firmware deals with the EISA bus?  I just don't know.

What I do know is that I'm not terribly satisfied with Digital's response
to the problem.  After looking at it a bit, they told us that they didn't
support third-party peripherals, nor were they interested in dealing with
whatever problems such devices might cause.

In one sense, I can understand their position.  But on the other hand, why
would a customer buy a second SCSI bus unless they were planning to install
some third-party SCSI devices?  And doesn't the fact that these same disks
work fine on such a wide variety of other systems (including many of DEC's
own machines!) suggest that there might be something rather marginal with
their EISA/Adaptec "solution" to providing the second SCSI bus?

I suppose it's possible that the drives might be slightly outside the SCSI
spec, and that something about the Digital/Adaptec design is a lot fussier
about requiring that things be within spec in order to work.  It would be
nice to find out whether any of the drives that we've seen problems with
(Seagate ST410800N and ST15150N, and Fujitsu M2949S) have a reputation for
being somewhat sloppy about conforming to the official SCSI specification.
Anyone have any information on that?

