In article <33D7CD73.2393A0B7@kpmg.com>, Hugh Belton  <hbelton@kpmg.com> wrote:
>I am new to Digital Unix and am having much trouble getting my 8400 to
>mount its cdrom.  I get messages saying no file system, or device busy.
>Nothing I've tried has helped.
>
>How do I get Digital Unix to identify which device is actually the
>cdrom?  I tried "file rz*c" but the results didn't tell me anything
>useful.

First, execute "file" on the raw disks

    file rrz*c

to find the SCSI CDROM drive.  Mine responds like this

    /dev/rrz4c:     character special (8/4098) SCSI #0 RRD45 disk #32  ...

Next, I always run a little script called mount-cd that tries to mount
the CD several ways:

   # try mounting a UFS CD
   mount -r /dev/rz4c /cdrom && exit
   # otherwise try mounting a CDFS CD
   mount -r -t cdfs -o rrip,noversion /dev/rz4c /cdrom && exit
   # Oops, nothing worked!
   echo "Could not mount CDROM!"

Hope this helps,
Andy St.Martin



