In article <33D7CD73.2393A0B7@kpmg.com>, Hugh Belton <hbelton@kpmg.com> writes:
>
>I am new to Digital Unix and am having much trouble getting my 8400 to
>mount its cdrom.  I get messages saying no file system, or device busy.
>Nothing I've tried has helped.

In addition to what Andy said, if file(1) doesn't find the CD, you
can try scu(8).  It will allow you to scan each SCSI bus and then
look to see what devices were found.  On V4.0 you don't need to
specify each bus, but before you do:

# scu scan edt [ bus # ]
# scu show edt [ bus # ] | grep "Read-Only"

If this doesn't find the CDROM, then you don't have one, or you
have a bigger problem than just mounting a CD.  Using the bus
target and LUN number from the scu(8) listing whatever CD shows
up, you can check to see if the special files actually exist
for it.  If not use /dev/MAKEDEV to create them:

# cd /dev
# ./MAKEDEV rz#

where # is (bus * 8) + target-id.  If the CDROM is not at LUN
zero (on an HSZ40 for example), the command is slightly different:

# ./MAKEDEV rzx#

where # is the same as before and the 'x' is a letter corresponding
to the logical unit number; LUN 1 = b, LUN 2 = c and so on.

Also, if the CDROM is ISO-9660 format, you need to make sure
your system has CDFS support.  Look at the configuration to see
if it has "options CDFS".

Finally, when asking questions such as this please try to provide
more information; version of the operating system, what kind of
CD, how it is connected, transcriptions of the command you tried
and the full error messages.

--
Alan Rollow				alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com




