Greg Coleman <gcoleman@adelphia.net> writes:

> 
> Hi
> Its my first experience with a digital ALPHA 600 series and 
> OSF/1. ALL I did was choose basic install off of the 3.2c CDROM.
> When the install process eventually reached the "Build Kernel"
> portion, the build will fail with "ld: unresolved externals"
> and go on to name a number of symbols all beginning with ace___.
> This was just a basic boot from CDROM and follow the instructions.
> I guess I missed one. Anyone know what it is? 
> 
> Please mail me at greg@crusoe.net.
> 
> Thanks
> Greg C.

I think that your problem is that the firmware of the 600 has
forgotten aout its serial ports. When you build a new kernel, a
description macro (the 'CPU' line in /sys/conf/MACHINE) is chosen
depending on the type of machine you're running. Amongst other this
macro will define the presence of serial ports (the 600 undeniably
_has_ serial ports doesn't it?). The problem is that, when the kernel
config moves on to gathering data about attached devices it won't be
able to probe the serial ports, thus excluding _most_ but not all
references to them in the kernel environment. This leads up to some
kind of inconsistence when you come to linking the kernel.

You can solve the problem in two ways - by a kludge or by a real
solution. The kludge consists of building the kernel on another
similar machine and ignore the fact that the serial ports don't work.

The solution means that you should run the ECU from the boot monitor
(SRM) and configure the serial from there. If this is successful, you
should be able to build a new kernel. ECU as a uility that comes on
floppy and is delivered along with the system.

Hope this helps.

Regards,
/Erik

