I don't know if this answer has been posted yet.  Here goes:

	Become root and type the command "swapon -s".  This lists
	all swap devices and how much used/free space they have.

	"swapon" is part of DUnix (it is in v3.2d and v4.0b, at least).
	swapon is normally used to add in swap devices.  Man page has
	more details.

	"top" is a great tool.  You should get it and compile it.
	In this case, "swapon" probably provides more information.

