I'm not sure where OSF1 V2.0 is specifically involved here.

I have installed 8505XL support under V3 .
As nearly as I can tell, there is no recommended association between the 
l/m/h/a device suffixes and the 8200//8200c/8500/8500c modes of the drive.
Most managers will pick 'l' as meaning 'low', or 8200 mode,
and will pick 'h' as 'high' or 8500c compressed mode.

( There are rare exceptions like me who don't want uninformed users writing
  compressed tapes, therefore picking the 'a' (alto?) suffix for 8500c mode,
  using h for straight 8500. )

In short: you cannot read the tape at all, so it is almost certainly
written in compressed mode. Note that you do not need any software support
to read the compressed tape, as the drive always reads the tape as it was
written. Of course, you do need a compression-capable drive.



