In article <33C259C8.167E@iplab2out.sdstate.edu>, Adam Fausch <fausch@iplab2out.sdstate.edu> writes:

>I'm running a DEC Alpha 600 on a unix operating system. currently We
>have seven devices on the narrow SCSI port and are triing to run a wide
>port also. How ever our unix is unable to ever find the wide drive.

Digital UNIX doesn't support wide addressing (wide transfers yes, but
not the extra address bit you get on a wide bus).  If the wide drive
is using an address above 7 then it won't be found.

Does the console see the device?  Until it does, there's usually
no point in having an operating system look for it.  If the console
does, does the operating system know about the 2nd bus.  I'd assume
so, but I've never see the boot messages for an AlphaStation 600
to see that it has two-built busses.  If the wide bus is a recent
add-on you have to update the configuration and rebuild the kernel
so that it know to look for the device.

Finally, what are you using to look for the device?  The most reliable
utility is scu(8).  You can force a scan of a bus and then show what
it found on that bus:

	scu scan edt bus 1
	scu show edt bus 1

>
