In article <33B813CF.C55822AE@starnet.com>, Steven Schoch <sschoch@starnet.com> writes:
>
>I'm running DEC Unix 4.0 with a "DEC AS10" 2.1 GB drive (at least that's
>what
>it's SCSI info says).

I don't know of any "AS10" that we've ever made.  You can use scu(8)
to look at the Inquiry data to verify.  It may not matter much.

>Is there a way to map out these bad blocks?

Use the "reassign lba" command in scu(8).  If the drive supports the
SCSI command to reassign blocks that will do it.  You can also make
a scan, which may get the drive or the driver to replace any additional
bad blocks.  And if all else fails, you can use scu(8) to tell the
disk to format itself.

--
Alan Rollow				alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com




