In regards to Unix 4.0b and DEC cluster software.  We have 2 Alpha servers,
2100 and 4100.  Both have FDDI cards and are connected to a FDDI ring (3COM
Lanplex switch).  This cluster is relatively new (4 months now).  Our
cluster implementation is basic failover cluster implementation (4100 fails
to the 2100).  We had a problem where the Lanplex switch/FDDI ring was
rebooted and it was unavailable for 2-3 minutes.  The 4100 sensed that the
network was unavailable, tried to shutdown Oracle (we had a problem with
the Oracle shutdown script) and tried to dump its cluster service to the
2100.  The 4100 then rebooted itself.  The 2100 could not see the network
either - so my question is why dump your cluster service to the failover
box when it can't get on the network either.  Somehow the cluster was
confused and we had to boot both Alphas.

Now my other question is:  If the LAN people need to work on the LANPLEX
switch/ FDDI ring or reboot it for 1-2 minutes.  Is there anyway to stop
the 4100 from detecting failover and dumping its cluster service.  I heard
there is a timeout value (45 seconds?) where if the cluster service can't
talk to the other failover box it tries to dump its cluster service.  Can I
temporarily change this timeout to 10 minutes or so???  So FDDI ring
maintenance can occur without 4100 shutdown problems.  Or can I temporarily
change some UNIX script file to prevent the cluster service from sensing
availability?

Jeff Shervey - (jeff.shervey@us.landisstaefa.com)



