Andreas was right to say that Digital had a 'clean' slate when they 
introduced Alpha.

It seemed at the time that a new 64-bit computer architecture would 
already run a different instruction set, and therefore be incompatible 
with the previous VAX and MIPS machines.

Digital did produce excellent binary translators. I used the mx product 
to translate Visual Numeric's (Then Precision Visual's) PV-Wave product 
from Ultrix to OSF/1. It worked like a dream. Most of the time we gave 
demonstrations, we didn't need to say it was a translation until 
afterwards! 

Andreas: I think that you are being a little unfair highlighting Sun as a 
vendor that plays fairer with it's Customers. It seems likely that Sun's 
commitment to SPARC has been persuasively explained to you. No doubt the 
same salesman also tried to spread Fear, Uncertainty & Doubt about 
Digital, HP, or IBM's commitment to the market.

I now work for Silicon Graphics, having already worked for Digital. I 
don't have a crystal ball, and wouldn't be able to say whether 
specialised  UNIX manufacturers like Sun or Silicon Graphics, or big 
diversified manufacturers like HP, Digital or IBM will win. What I can 
say for certain is that there are only two manufacturers now that have 
offered a complete 64-bit environment for the last 2 years, and that you 
can guarantee the minimum of disruption by using them instead of a 
company offering a 32bit or partial 32/64 bit solution.

Mark Jeffery
My opinions only!



