>   <>    Eric (Ric) Werme   <>      This space under reconstruction       <>
>   <>  <werme@zk3.dec.com>  <>                                            <>

A solution has been found and it lies in the  mount  command used.
Eric Werme was partially correct in that the  nfsv2  parameter is
required.  The other parameter required is  port=2049.  The use of
nfsv2  or  port=2049  alone is not enough.  Thus the command:

 mount -t nfs -o rw,nfsv2,port=2049 Pluto:/c /mnt

is that required.  From the man page for  mount, the  wsize  and
rsize  buffer parameters are filled in by the kernel if not specified
and the values used are possibly 512 for each.  When I accessed
TIFF graphics files across the NFS link, then it was necessary to
explicitly specify values of 1024 for the  wsize  and  rsize  buffers.

This mount command and the above comments also apply to the
shareware Tropic NSF server that I obtained from

ftp://archie.au/pc/coast/win95/winsock/trn3230b.zip

which performs the same file servicing function under Windows NT as
that done by SOSS under MS-DOS, both executing on a PC.

Ross
